Trackflow Website Test Cases

1. Accessibility Tests

ACC-001: Semantic HTML Structure

Objective: Verify that the page uses meaningful HTML elements.

Steps:

Inspect the page structure.

Check for a <header>, <nav>, <main>, and <footer> where appropriate.

Check that headings follow a logical hierarchy.

Expected result: Content has meaningful structure and headings are not selected solely for their visual appearance.

Priority: High

ACC-002: Contact Form Labels

Objective: Ensure every form field has an accessible name.

Steps:

Inspect every input, select, and textarea.

Confirm that each field has an associated <label> or another valid accessible name.

Verify that label associations use matching for and id attributes when applicable.

Expected result: Users can identify every field without relying on placeholder text.

Priority: Critical

ACC-003: Keyboard Navigation

Objective: Verify that interactive elements work without a mouse.

Steps:

Navigate the page using Tab and Shift+Tab.

Activate buttons and links using the keyboard.

Complete the contact form using the keyboard.

Expected result: Focus is visible, navigation order is logical, and controls can be operated using the keyboard.

Priority: Critical

ACC-004: Image Alternatives

Objective: Verify that meaningful images have appropriate text alternatives.

Steps:

Inspect all <img> elements.

Check that informative images have meaningful alt text.

Check that purely decorative images use an empty alt="".

Expected result: Images are understandable to users of assistive technology without redundant descriptions.

Priority: High

ACC-005: Color Contrast and Focus

Objective: Check visual accessibility.

Steps:

Measure text and background contrast.

Check links, buttons, form errors, and keyboard focus indicators.

Expected result: Normal text generally meets a contrast ratio of 4.5:1; large text generally meets 3:1. Applicable controls and focus indicators are also sufficiently distinguishable.

Priority: High

ACC-006: Accessible Form Errors

Objective: Ensure validation errors can be identified by all users.

Steps:

Submit the form with missing or invalid values.

Inspect the error messages and affected fields.

Check whether errors are programmatically associated with the fields and whether invalid fields expose their state.

Expected result: Errors are specific, understandable, and not communicated through color alone.

Priority: Critical

2. Responsive Design Tests

RES-001: Mobile Layout

Objective: Verify usability on narrow screens.

Steps:

Test at approximately 320px, 375px, and 390px viewport widths.

Inspect navigation, headings, content sections, cards, buttons, and forms.

Expected result: Content remains readable, controls remain usable, and no unintended horizontal page scrolling occurs.

Priority: Critical

RES-002: Tablet Layout

Objective: Verify layout behavior at tablet widths.

Steps:

Test at approximately 768px and 1024px widths.

Inspect columns, spacing, navigation, and form alignment.

Expected result: Components adapt without overlapping or leaving large unintended gaps.

Priority: High

RES-003: Desktop Layout

Objective: Verify the full desktop experience.

Steps:

Test at approximately 1280px and 1440px widths.

Inspect content alignment, readable line lengths, and section spacing.

Expected result: The layout remains consistent and professional across desktop sizes.

Priority: High

RES-004: Responsive Navigation

Objective: Verify that navigation adapts to smaller screens.

Steps:

Test the navigation at mobile, tablet, and desktop widths.

If a mobile menu exists, open and close it using pointer and keyboard input.

Expected result: Navigation remains accessible, usable, and free of overlapping content.

Priority: High

RES-005: Zoom and Text Scaling

Objective: Check whether content remains usable when enlarged.

Steps:

Zoom the browser to 200%.

Inspect navigation, content, buttons, and form controls.

Expected result: Content remains available without clipping or loss of functionality, subject to applicable WCAG requirements.

Priority: High

3. Form Validation Tests

FORM-001: Empty Submission

Objective: Prevent submission when required fields are missing.

Steps:

Leave required fields empty.

Submit the contact form.

Expected result: Submission is blocked or rejected with clear, accessible validation feedback.

Priority: Critical

FORM-002: Invalid Email Address

Objective: Reject malformed email addresses.

Steps:

Enter an invalid email such as invalid-email.

Submit the form.

Expected result: The form identifies the invalid email and does not accept the submission.

Priority: Critical

FORM-003: Valid Form Submission

Objective: Verify the intended successful submission flow.

Steps:

Enter valid values in all required fields.

Submit the form.

Inspect the actual request and response if a backend is configured.

Expected result: Data reaches the configured submission endpoint, success is shown only after confirmation, and users receive a clear confirmation message.

Priority: Critical

FORM-004: Server or Network Failure

Objective: Verify behavior when submission fails.

Steps:

Simulate an unavailable endpoint or failed network request.

Submit valid form data.

Expected result: The user receives an error message, their entered data is preserved where practical, and the interface does not falsely report success.

Priority: Critical

FORM-005: Duplicate Submission

Objective: Avoid accidental duplicate requests.

Steps:

Submit the form.

Attempt to submit it repeatedly while the request is processing.

Expected result: Repeated submissions are appropriately controlled, and duplicate requests are prevented where required.

Priority: High

FORM-006: Input Boundaries and Unexpected Text

Objective: Check input handling and security.

Steps:

Test empty strings, long values, whitespace, punctuation, and unexpected characters.

Test text resembling HTML or script code in text fields.

Expected result: Inputs are handled safely, length limits are enforced where appropriate, and user-provided content is not executed as code.

Priority: Critical

FORM-007: Server-Side Validation

Objective: Confirm that validation is not limited to browser-side checks.

Steps:

Inspect the form submission endpoint or backend implementation.

Test invalid requests against the endpoint in an authorized test environment.

Expected result: The backend independently validates fields and rejects invalid or unauthorized requests.

Priority: Critical

4. Code Quality Tests

CODE-001: HTML Validity

Objective: Detect invalid HTML structure.

Steps:

Inspect element nesting and closing tags.

Check for duplicate IDs and invalid attributes.

Run an HTML validator.

Expected result: No significant markup errors remain.

Priority: High

CODE-002: JavaScript Reliability

Objective: Detect runtime errors.

Steps:

Load the page.

Interact with navigation and the contact form.

Inspect the browser console.

Expected result: No unhandled exceptions or unexpected console errors occur.

Priority: High

CODE-003: CSS and Responsive Rules

Objective: Check style consistency and maintainability.

Steps:

Inspect stylesheets and responsive breakpoints.

Look for conflicting rules, unintended overrides, and broken layouts.

Expected result: Styles are organized, responsive, and free of significant layout defects.

Priority: Medium

CODE-004: Dependencies and External Resources

Objective: Identify dependency and loading problems.

Steps:

Inspect external scripts, stylesheets, fonts, and image resources.

Check for failed requests and unnecessary dependencies.

Review the use of third-party scripts.

Expected result: Required resources load successfully, and dependencies are justified and appropriately maintained.

Priority: High

CODE-005: Security and Data Handling

Objective: Verify safe handling of user data.

Steps:

Inspect form handling and any backend integration.

Check for unsafe HTML insertion, exposed secrets, and sensitive data in client-side code.

Verify HTTPS and appropriate server-side protections in the deployed environment.

Expected result: No critical data-handling or client-side security issues are identified.

Priority: Critical

CODE-006: Maintainability

Objective: Ensure the source code is understandable and reusable.

Steps:

Inspect naming conventions, duplicated logic, and file organization.

Check whether event handlers and validation logic are easy to locate.

Review comments and documentation for accuracy.

Expected result: Code is understandable, reasonably modular, and documented where necessary.

Priority: Medium

